  },
  "cover": {
    "hook": "A pair of Air Jordans costs the same in every shop, yet some months they sell out and some months they sit on the shelf. Work out why and you can say which way demand will move.",
    "picture": "cover",
    "question": "What makes people want more Air Jordans?",
  },
  "goals": [
    "Why each extra pair is worth less to you",
    "Moving along the demand curve, and shifting it",
    "The six things that shift demand: PETPIG",
  ],
  "id": "demand",
  "label": "What shifts demand",
  "lesson": [
    {
      "part": "Why demand slopes down",
      "reveal": [
        "pair1",
      ],
      "say": "Say you've wanted a pair of Air Jordans for ages. What's the most you'd pay for your first pair this year? Say $400.

That's what the first pair is worth to you, so it goes on the chart at 1 pair and $400.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "The satisfaction you get from having or using something.",
        "word": "Utility",
      },
      "title": "Your first pair",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "pair2",
      ],
      "say": "Now a second pair. A spare is good to have, but it isn't as exciting as the first. You'd pay $300 for it, tops.

The second pair adds less to your satisfaction than the first did.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "The extra satisfaction you get from one more of something.",
        "word": "Marginal utility",
      },
      "title": "Your second pair",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "pair3",
      ],
      "say": "A third pair? You'd wear them, but not as often. They're worth $200 to you.

Each pair adds less than the one before, so each is worth less to you.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "Each extra unit of something adds less satisfaction than the one before.",
        "word": "Diminishing marginal utility",
      },
      "title": "Your third pair",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "pair4",
      ],
      "say": "By the fourth pair you have more than you can wear: $100, tops.

Every pair is worth $100 less than the last. The more pairs you have, the less one more is worth, so your points step down.",
      "title": "Your fourth pair",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "priceLine",
        "priceSymbol",
      ],
      "say": "Nike charges $300 a pair: the dotted line, P. Your first two pairs are worth at least that to you, so you buy 2. Your third is worth only $200, so you don't buy it.

Move the price and count how many pairs you'd buy.",
      "set": {
        "price": 300,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "When the price of something falls, people buy more of it. When it rises, they buy less.",
        "word": "Law of demand",
      },
      "title": "Nike sets the price",
      "try": [
        {
          "label": "Nike's price",
          "param": "price",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "focus": "market",
      "part": "From you to the market",
      "reveal": [
        "market",
      ],
      "say": "You're one of about 100,000 Air Jordan fans in Australia, and each values a pair a bit differently. Add up the pairs they'd all buy at each price.

All those points together make one smooth line: the market demand curve, D.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 200,
        "price": 300,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "How much all buyers together are willing and able to buy at each price.",
        "word": "Market demand",
      },
      "title": "Every fan in Australia",
    },
    {
      "say": "At Nike's price, P = $300, fans want 200,000 pairs a year. Read it off the bottom axis, at Qd.

One price gives one quantity: a single point on the curve.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "How much buyers want to buy at one particular price.",
        "word": "Quantity demanded",
      },
      "title": "Quantity demanded",
    },
    {
      "say": "Say Nike cuts its price to $250. Fans want 250,000 pairs a year, and the point slides down the curve from the faint one, where it was. The curve itself doesn't move.

You still buy 2: your third pair is worth only $200.",
      "set": {
        "price": 250,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "A move along the demand curve, because the good's own price changed.",
        "word": "Change in quantity demanded",
      },
      "title": "Quantity demanded rises",
      "try": [
        {
          "label": "Nike's price",
          "param": "price",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "part": "PETPIG: what shifts demand",
      "say": "Back at $300, change anything other than Nike's price and the whole demand curve shifts. The six things that shift it spell PETPIG.

Prices of related goods, Expectations, Tastes, Population, Income and Government policy.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 200,
        "price": 300,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "The whole demand curve shifts, because something other than the good's own price changed.",
        "word": "Change in demand",
      },
      "title": "What shifts the whole curve",
    },
    {
      "say": "Adidas puts its sneakers on sale. Fans could buy them instead of Jordans, and now they're cheaper.

So fans want fewer Jordans at every price. Demand decreases: the curve shifts left of the dashed one, where it was, to 150,000 pairs at $300.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 150,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "A good bought instead of another, like Adidas sneakers instead of Air Jordans.",
        "word": "Substitute",
      },
      "title": "P: a substitute gets cheaper",
    },
    {
      "say": "Air Jordans began as basketball shoes. Say club basketball fees fall, so more people sign up to play.

Playing and the shoes go together, so fans want more Jordans at every price. Demand increases: the curve shifts right.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 200,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "A good used together with another, like basketball and basketball shoes.",
        "word": "Complement",
      },
      "title": "P: a complement gets cheaper",
    },
    {
      "say": "Fans hear that Air Jordans will be marked down in the Boxing Day sales, so many decide to wait.

They want fewer pairs now, at every price. Demand today decreases: the curve shifts left.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 150,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "What buyers think will happen later, such as a price rising or falling.",
        "word": "Expectations",
      },
      "title": "E: expectations",
    },
    {
      "say": "An NBA star wears a new pair of Jordans in the finals, and the clip goes viral.

Suddenly Jordans are the shoe everyone wants. Demand increases: the curve shifts right.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 200,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "What buyers like, which changes with fashion, advertising and who's wearing what.",
        "word": "Tastes and preferences",
      },
      "title": "T: tastes and preferences",
    },
    {
      "say": "Australia's population grows, with more teenagers and young adults: the people who buy the most Jordans.

More buyers want more pairs at every price, so demand increases. Your own points don't move: there are just more fans.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 250,
      },
      "title": "P: population",
    },
    {
      "say": "Wages rise, and fans have more to spend. Air Jordans are a normal good: people buy more of them as their income rises.

Demand increases again. At $300, fans now want 300,000 pairs a year.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 300,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "A good people buy more of when their income rises.",
        "word": "Normal good",
      },
      "title": "I: income",
    },
    {
      "say": "The last letter, G, is for government policies, such as taxes and subsidies. They're a lesson of their own, so we'll leave G there.

Everything in PETPIG shifts the whole curve. A change in Nike's own price only moves fans along it.",
      "title": "G: government policy",
    },
    {
      "aside": "wageNews",
      "ask": {
