exports[`the "fx" lesson > is the lesson it was before the kit 1`] = `
{
  "asides": {
    "rbaReport": {
      "body": "Investors overseas buy Australian bonds to earn the higher return.",
      "headline": "The RBA lifts the cash rate above US interest rates",
      "name": "News report",
      "note": "A made-up report, for this question",
      "picture": "rba",
    },
  },
  "cover": {
    "hook": "Every time the world buys Australian iron ore, a degree or a week on the Gold Coast, someone has to buy AUD to pay for it. Follow those trades and you can work out which way the AUD moves when the news changes.",
    "picture": "cover",
    "question": "Why does the Australian dollar rise and fall?",
  },
  "goals": [
    "Who buys Australian dollars, and who sells them",
    "Why the AUD rises and falls against the USD",
    "Four real-world cases to work out yourself",
  ],
  "id": "fx",
  "label": "Exchange rates (AUD)",
  "lesson": [
    {
      "part": "Meet the market",
      "picture": "trade",
      "say": "People overseas need AUD to pay for what Australia sells: iron ore, beef, wine, a degree at an Australian university, a holiday on the Gold Coast. Investors need AUD to buy Australian shares and property.",
      "title": "Who wants Australian dollars?",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "demand_market",
      ],
      "say": "The dearer the AUD, the more all of that costs them, so the less AUD they want. That's why the demand curve slopes down.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "How much AUD people want to buy at each exchange rate.",
        "word": "Demand for AUD",
      },
      "title": "Demand for AUD slopes down",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "supply_market",
      ],
      "say": "Australians do, whenever they pay for something from overseas: imported phones and cars, a holiday in Bali, shares in an American company. To pay, they swap AUD for foreign currency.

The more USD each AUD buys, the better the deal, so the more AUD they're willing to sell. That's why the supply curve slopes up.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "How much AUD people want to sell for other currencies at each exchange rate.",
        "word": "Supply of AUD",
      },
      "title": "Who sells Australian dollars?",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "equilibrium_market",
      ],
      "say": "Where the curves cross, the amount of AUD people want to buy matches the amount they want to sell. That sets the price of the AUD: AUD 1 = USD 0.65.

Nobody chooses this number. The AUD floats, so the market sets its price, the same way it sets the price of anything else.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "The price of one currency in another. Here, how many USD one AUD buys.",
        "word": "Exchange rate",
      },
      "title": "The exchange rate",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "mirror",
      ],
      "say": "Anyone buying AUD with USD is also selling USD. So demand for AUD and the supply of USD are the same trades, and the second panel shows them from the American side.

Read that way round, the price is USD 1 = AUD 1.54.",
      "title": "The same trades, from the US side",
    },
    {
      "part": "When the AUD moves",
      "say": "Say iron ore prices soar. The world spends more on Australian ore, so it wants more AUD at every exchange rate.

The whole demand curve shifts to the right. Watch where the curves cross now.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 60,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "When something other than the exchange rate changes how much AUD people want, and the whole curve moves.",
        "word": "Shift",
      },
      "title": "Demand for AUD increases",
    },
    {
      "say": "AUD 1 now buys USD 0.70, so the AUD has appreciated: it's worth more than before.

From the US side, USD 1 now costs AUD 1.43. The USD has depreciated: it's worth less.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "A currency appreciates when it's worth more of another currency, and depreciates when it's worth less.",
        "word": "Appreciate and depreciate",
      },
      "title": "The AUD appreciates",
    },
    {
      "say": "Up the price axis, the AUD appreciates. Down it, the AUD depreciates.

Iron ore prices settle, demand goes back to where it was, and so does the rate: AUD 1 = USD 0.65.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 50,
      },
      "title": "Up is stronger, down is weaker",
    },
    {
      "say": "Now say Australians travel overseas in record numbers. Every trip means swapping AUD for foreign currency, so Australians sell more AUD at every exchange rate.

This time it's the supply curve that shifts to the right.",
      "set": {
        "supply": 60,
      },
      "title": "Supply of AUD increases",
    },
    {
      "say": "AUD 1 now buys only USD 0.60, so the AUD has depreciated.

From the US side, USD 1 now costs AUD 1.67. The USD has appreciated.",
      "title": "The AUD depreciates",
    },
    {
      "aside": "rbaReport",
      "ask": {
        "among": [
          "demand",
          "supply",
        ],
        "direction": "up",
        "explain": "To buy Australian bonds, investors first need AUD. So demand for AUD increases, the demand curve shifts right, and the AUD appreciates.",
        "illustrate": 10,
        "param": "demand",
      },
      "part": "Your turn",
      "say": "Read the news report next to the diagram.

Which curve moves, and which way? Move it, then press Check.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 50,
        "supply": 50,
      },
      "title": "Interest rates rise",
    },
    {
      "ask": {
        "among": [
          "demand",
          "supply",
        ],
        "direction": "up",
        "explain": "To pay for imports, Australians sell AUD for foreign currency. So the supply of AUD increases, the supply curve shifts right, and the AUD depreciates.",
        "illustrate": 10,
        "param": "supply",
      },
      "say": "Prices in Australia rise faster than in the US. Imports now look cheap next to Australian-made goods, so Australians switch to imports.

Which curve moves, and which way?",
      "set": {
        "demand": 50,
        "supply": 50,
      },
      "title": "Prices rise faster in Australia",
    },
    {
      "ask": {
        "among": [
          "demand",
          "supply",
        ],
        "direction": "down",
        "explain": "Less ore bought means less AUD needed to pay for it. So demand for AUD decreases, the demand curve shifts left, and the AUD depreciates.",
        "illustrate": 10,
        "param": "demand",
      },
      "say": "China's steel mills cut production and buy less Australian iron ore.

Which curve moves, and which way?",
      "set": {
        "demand": 50,
        "supply": 50,
      },
      "title": "China buys less iron ore",
    },
    {
      "ask": {
        "among": [
          "demand",
          "supply",
        ],
        "direction": "down",
        "explain": "Fewer imports and trips abroad mean less AUD swapped for foreign currency. So the supply of AUD decreases, the supply curve shifts left, and the AUD appreciates.",
        "illustrate": 10,
        "param": "supply",
      },
      "say": "In a downturn, Australians buy fewer imported cars and holiday at home instead.

Which curve moves, and which way?",
      "set": {
        "demand": 50,
        "supply": 50,
      },
      "title": "Australians spend less overseas",
    },
    {
      "recap": {
        "takeaway": "Anything that changes how much AUD the world wants to buy, or how much AUD Australians want to sell, moves the exchange rate: more demand or less supply and the AUD appreciates, the reverse and it depreciates.",
      },
      "say": "Here's how you went on the four cases.

Press Finish to take this market to the Playground, where you can try more real-world cases in Scenarios or drag the curves yourself.",
      "set": {
        "demand": 50,
        "supply": 50,
      },
      "title": "Over to you",
    },
  ],
  "model": {
    "diagram": {
      "calcs": {
        "Qe": "(params.demand + params.supply)/2",
        "dInverse": "calcs.inverse - prev.calcs.inverse",
        "dRate": "calcs.rate - prev.calcs.rate",
        "inverse": "1/calcs.rate",
        "rate": "0.65 + (params.demand - params.supply)/200",
      },
      "layout": {
        "CustomLayout": {
          "panels": [
            {
              "key": "market",
              "objects": [
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.demand",
                    "drag": [
                      {
                        "draggable": "not(params.submitted)",
                        "horizontal": "demand",
                      },
                    ],
                    "fn": "params.demand - 100*((y) - 0.65)",
                    "ghost": {
                      "label": "_0",
                      "show": "not(params.asking)",
                    },
                    "label": {
                      "text": "D",
                      "y": 0.47,
                    },
                    "name": "demand_market",
                    "samplePoints": 2,
                    "srTitle": "Demand for AUD",
                    "title": "demand for AUD",
                  },
                  "type": "Curve",
                },
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.supply",
                    "drag": [
                      {
                        "draggable": "not(params.submitted)",
                        "horizontal": "supply",
                      },
                    ],
                    "fn": "params.supply + 100*((y) - 0.65)",
                    "ghost": {
                      "label": "_0",
                      "show": "not(params.asking)",
                    },
                    "label": {
                      "text": "S",
                      "y": 0.86,
                    },
                    "name": "supply_market",
                    "samplePoints": 2,
                    "srTitle": "Supply of AUD",
                    "title": "supply of AUD",
                  },
                  "type": "Curve",
                },
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.demand",
                    "fn": "params.startDemand - 100*((y) - 0.65)",
                    "lineStyle": "dashed",
                    "name": "questionStartDemand",
                    "samplePoints": 2,
                    "show": "params.asking",
                    "strokeOpacity": 0.35,
                  },
                  "type": "Curve",
                },
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.supply",
                    "fn": "params.startSupply + 100*((y) - 0.65)",
                    "lineStyle": "dashed",
                    "name": "questionStartSupply",
                    "samplePoints": 2,
                    "show": "params.asking",
                    "strokeOpacity": 0.35,
                  },
                  "type": "Curve",
                },
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.demand",
                    "fn": "params.answerDemand - 100*((y) - 0.65)",
                    "label": {
                      "text": "D_1",
                      "y": 0.47,
                    },
                    "lineStyle": "dotted",
                    "name": "questionAnswerDemand",
                    "samplePoints": 2,
                    "show": "params.revealed and (params.answerDemand != params.startDemand)",
                  },
                  "type": "Curve",
                },
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.supply",
                    "fn": "params.answerSupply + 100*((y) - 0.65)",
                    "label": {
                      "text": "S_1",
                      "y": 0.86,
                    },
                    "lineStyle": "dotted",
                    "name": "questionAnswerSupply",
                    "samplePoints": 2,
                    "show": "params.revealed and (params.answerSupply != params.startSupply)",
                  },
                  "type": "Curve",
                },
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.equilibriumPrice",
                    "droplines": {
                      "horizontal": "e^*",
                      "vertical": "Q^*",
                    },
                    "ghost": true,
                    "name": "equilibrium_market",
                    "srTitle": "Equilibrium exchange rate",
                    "title": "equilibrium",
                    "x": "calcs.Qe",
                    "y": "calcs.rate",
                  },
                  "type": "Point",
                },
              ],
              "xAxis": {
                "intercept": 0.4,
                "max": 100,
                "min": 0,
                "title": "Quantity of AUD traded",
              },
              "yAxis": {
                "max": 0.9,
                "min": 0.4,
                "tickPrecision": 2,
                "title": "USD per AUD",
              },
            },
            {
              "key": "mirror",
              "objects": [
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.supply",
                    "drag": [
                      {
                        "draggable": "not(params.submitted)",
                        "horizontal": "demand",
                      },
                    ],
                    "fn": "params.demand - 100*(1/(y) - 0.65)",
                    "ghost": {
                      "label": "_0",
                      "show": "not(params.asking)",
                    },
                    "label": {
