exports[`the "market" lesson > is the lesson it was before the kit 1`] = `
{
  "cover": {
    "hook": "Nobody sets most prices. They settle where what buyers want meets what sellers will sell, and when either side changes the price moves, and with it who gains.",
    "question": "Why do prices go up and down?",
  },
  "goals": [
    "How demand and supply set a price",
    "What changes when demand rises",
    "One change to work out yourself",
  ],
  "id": "market",
  "label": "Supply and demand",
  "lesson": [
    {
      "part": "Meet the market",
      "reveal": [
        "demand_market",
      ],
      "say": "The demand curve, D, shows how much of something buyers want at each price.

It slopes down. When the price goes up, people buy less, and when it comes down, they buy more.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "How much buyers are willing and able to buy at each price.",
        "word": "Demand",
      },
      "title": "Demand: what buyers want",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "supply_market",
      ],
      "say": "The supply curve, S, shows how much sellers want to sell at each price.

It slopes up. A higher price makes it worth producing more.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "How much sellers are willing and able to sell at each price.",
        "word": "Supply",
      },
      "title": "Supply: what sellers offer",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "equilibrium_market",
      ],
      "say": "Where the curves cross, the amount buyers want is exactly the amount sellers offer. Nothing is left over, and nobody who will pay that price misses out.

That point gives one price, P*, and one quantity, Q*.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "The price and quantity where demand equals supply.",
        "word": "Equilibrium",
      },
      "title": "Where they meet",
    },
    {
      "part": "When demand changes",
      "reveal": [
        "surplus",
      ],
      "say": "Lots of buyers would have paid more than the market price. The shaded area adds up that extra value, for every buyer.

It's the same market, shown a second way.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "The difference between what buyers would be willing to pay and what they actually pay.",
        "word": "Consumer surplus",
      },
      "title": "What buyers gain",
    },
    {
      "say": "Say people's incomes rise. Buyers can afford more, so they want more at every price, and the whole demand curve shifts to the right.

Watch the price, the quantity and the shaded area all change together.",
      "set": {
        "a": 26,
      },
      "term": {
        "meaning": "Buyers want more at every price, so the demand curve shifts right.",
        "word": "Increase in demand",
      },
      "title": "Incomes rise",
    },
    {
      "reveal": [
        "revenue",
      ],
      "say": "The new panel shows the money that changes hands: the price times the quantity sold.

When demand rises, sellers sell more, at a higher price, so their revenue goes up. Move demand yourself and watch revenue follow it.",
      "term": {
        "meaning": "Price × quantity: everything buyers spend, and sellers receive.",
        "word": "Total revenue",
      },
      "title": "What sellers earn",
      "try": [
        {
          "label": "Demand",
          "param": "a",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "say": "Incomes settle back, and the market returns to where it began.

All three panels are on screen now: the market, what buyers gain, and what sellers earn.",
      "set": {
        "a": 20,
        "c": 2,
      },
      "title": "Back to the start",
    },
    {
      "ask": {
        "direction": "up",
        "explain": "Higher incomes mean buyers want more at every price, so demand increases and the curve shifts right — as far as it went last time, which takes the price to $14.00.",
        "param": "a",
        "target": 26,
        "tolerance": 1,
      },
      "part": "Your turn",
      "say": "Incomes rise again, by the same amount as before.

Move the demand curve to where it goes, then press Check.",
      "set": {
        "a": 20,
        "c": 2,
      },
      "title": "Incomes rise again",
    },
    {
      "say": "One shift in demand moved the price, the quantity, consumer surplus and revenue, all at once.

Press Finish and the market is yours in the Playground: drag the demand curve, try a scenario, or see the maths behind each number.",
      "set": {
        "a": 20,
        "c": 2,
      },
      "title": "Over to you",
    },
  ],
  "model": {
    "diagram": {
      "calcs": {
        "CS": "0.5 * calcs.Qe * (params.a - calcs.Pe)",
        "Pe": "(params.a + params.c)/2",
        "Qe": "(params.a - params.c)/2",
        "TR": "calcs.Pe * calcs.Qe",
        "dCS": "calcs.CS - prev.calcs.CS",
        "dPe": "calcs.Pe - prev.calcs.Pe",
        "dTR": "calcs.TR - prev.calcs.TR",
      },
      "layout": {
        "CustomLayout": {
          "panels": [
            {
              "key": "market",
              "objects": [
                {
                  "def": {
                    "color": "colors.demand",
                    "drag": [
                      {
